{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Come, let me counsel you</b> – Since he wished to declare the calamity destined for his nation, as it is written, \"and shall crush the borders of Moab\" (Numbers 24:17), therefore he said to him, \"let me counsel you,\" meaning: I tell you privately, in the manner of counsel, so that others will not hear. \"Come, let me counsel you\" — what you should do. And what is the counsel? Their Elohim hates licentiousness, etc., as is found in the chapter Chelek (Sanhedrin 106a). You may know that Balaam gave them this counsel through licentiousness, for it is said, \"Behold, these caused the children of Israel, through the counsel of Balaam\" (Numbers 31:16). <b>What this people will do to your people</b> – It is an elliptical verse: \"I will counsel you to make them stumble,\" and I will tell you what is destined to befall Moab in the end of days; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 24:14). And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he says to him: I will tell you the counsel that Elohim has devised, which this people will do to your people in the end of days — in the language of \"this is the counsel that is counseled against the whole earth\" (Isaiah 14:26), \"hear the counsel of YHWH that He has counseled against Edom\" (Jeremiah 49:20). And he said \"let me counsel you,\" because one who hears counsel is called one who takes counsel. Now this prophecy pertains to the days of the Messiah, for all his prophecies progressively add regarding the future. First he said that they are the portion of YHWH and His inheritance; in the second he added the conquest of the land and the slaying of their kings; in the third he saw their dwelling in the land, that they multiplied and increased upon the earth, and he established for them a king who would defeat Agag, and that the kingdom he saw — of David — would be exalted on high, as it is said, \"And David knew that YHWH had established him king over Israel, and that He had exalted His kingdom for the sake of His people Israel\" (II Samuel 5:12), meaning: for the sake of that which He promised them, that their kingdom would be exalted. And now, in this fourth prophecy, he adds to see the matter of the Messiah, and therefore he placed the matter very far off and said, \"I see him, but not now; I behold him, but not near\" (Numbers 24:17), which he did not say in the earlier prophecies. And he said that this is the counsel of YHWH that He devised to come to pass in the end of days:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The closing colon after the final word indicates the Ramban's comment continues directly into the scriptural verse that follows, 'I see him but not now' (Numbers 24:17)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:31.567159039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}